From part (d), the upper branch of the Hadley circulation has conserved angular momentum. In the northern winter hemisphere the air moving poleward aloft therefore acquires an eastward velocity relative to the Earth, while in the southern hemisphere it acquires a westward velocity. Near the surface, however, friction nearly balances the Coriolis force in the east-west direction. Hence the east-west component of the friction force must be opposite to the Coriolis force: eastward in the Northern Hemisphere and westward in the Southern Hemisphere.
\nBecause friction opposes the motion, the near-surface wind must therefore blow in the opposite directions: westward in the Northern Hemisphere and eastward in the Southern Hemisphere, with flow toward the equator from both sides. Thus the tropical near-surface pattern during northern winter solstice is an equatorward cross-equatorial flow with easterlies in the north and westerlies in the south.
\nFinal answer: \boxed{\text{westward near the surface in the Northern Hemisphere and eastward near the surface in the Southern Hemisphere, both toward the equator}}

The image monopoles in the upper region contribute a magnetic potential of the form
\[
\Phi_+(x,z)=\frac{\mu_0 q_m}{4\pi}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\left[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{(x+n v\tau)^2+(z+h+n v_0\tau)^2}}
-
\frac{1}{\sqrt{(x+(n+1)v\tau)^2+(z+h+n v_0\tau)^2}}
\right].
\]
\nFor the motion stated in the problem, the dipole moves horizontally at constant height, so \(v_0=0\). Then the series telescopes:
\[
\Phi_+(x,z)=\frac{\mu_0 q_m}{4\pi}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}
\left[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{(x+n v\tau)^2+(z+h)^2}}
-
\frac{1}{\sqrt{(x+(n+1)v\tau)^2+(z+h)^2}}
\right]
=\frac{\mu_0 q_m}{4\pi}\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+(z+h)^2}}.
\]
\nTherefore,
\[
\boxed{\Phi_+(x,z)=\frac{\mu_0 q_m}{4\pi\sqrt{x^2+(z+h)^2}}}
\].

