We know the orbital period of Mars then the angle $\angle M_{1}SM_{2}$ can be determined simply

$$
\angle M_{1} S M_{2} = \frac{106}{687} \times 360 = 55.5^{\circ}
$$

By the same way we determine $\angle E_1SE_2$:

$$
\angle E_{1} S E_{2} = \frac{106}{365} \times 360 = 104.5^{\circ}
$$

Then angle $\angle M_2SE_2$ is:

$$
\angle M_{2} S E_{2} = 104.5 - 55.5 = 49.0^{\circ}
$$

$$
\cos (\angle M_{2} S E_{2}) = \frac{S E_{2}}{S M_{2}}
$$

$$
r_{\mathrm{mars}} = \frac{S M_{2}}{S E_{2}} = \frac{1}{\cos (\angle M_{2} S E_{2})} = \frac{1}{\cos (49^{\circ})} = 1.52\ \mathrm{AU}
$$

\boxed{1.52\ \mathrm{AU}}

