solution:
By definition, when the star culminates in Lisbon, its hour angle is exactly $0^{\circ}$. Therefore its hour angle in Thessaloniki is $0^{\circ} + (\lambda_{1} - \lambda_{2})$. Compute $\lambda_{1} - \lambda_{2} = 1^{\mathrm{h}}32^{\mathrm{m}} - (-0^{\mathrm{h}}36^{\mathrm{m}}) = 2^{\mathrm{h}}08^{\mathrm{m}}$. Thus $H = 2^{\mathrm{h}}08^{\mathrm{m}}$. 

Using the cosine law for spherical triangles: $\cos z = \cos(90^{\circ} - \varphi_{1}) \cos(90^{\circ} - d) + \sin(90^{\circ} - \varphi_{1}) \sin(90^{\circ} - d) \cos H$. Here $90^{\circ} - \varphi_{1} = 49^{\circ}23'$, $90^{\circ} - d = 51^{\circ}13'$, and $H = 2^{\mathrm{h}}08^{\mathrm{m}} = 32^{\circ}$ (since $1^{\mathrm{h}} = 15^{\circ}$). Then $\cos z = \cos 49^{\circ}23' \cos 51^{\circ}13' + \sin 49^{\circ}23' \sin 51^{\circ}13' \cos 32^{\circ}$. Evaluating: $\cos 49^{\circ}23' \approx 0.6511$, $\cos 51^{\circ}13' \approx 0.6266$, $\sin 49^{\circ}23' \approx 0.7590$, $\sin 51^{\circ}13' \approx 0.7794$, $\cos 32^{\circ} \approx 0.8480$. So $\cos z \approx 0.6511 \times 0.6266 + 0.7590 \times 0.7794 \times 0.8480 \approx 0.4080 + 0.5016 = 0.9096$. Hence $z \approx \arccos(0.9096) \approx 24^{\circ}33'$. 

\boxed{H = 2^{\mathrm{h}}08^{\mathrm{m}}}, \boxed{z = 24^{\circ}33'}

