The blocks slide relative to the prism with accelerations $a_{1}$ and $a_{2}$, which are parallel to its sides and have the same magnitude $a$. The blocks move relative to the earth with accelerations:

(1.1) $\mathbf{w}_1 = \mathbf{a}_1 + \mathbf{a}_0$;  
(1.2) $\mathbf{w}_2 = \mathbf{a}_2 + \mathbf{a}_0$.

Projecting $\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\mathbf{w}_2$ along the $x$- and $y$-axes:

(1.3) $w_{1x} = a\cos \alpha_{1} - a_{0}$;  
(1.4) $w_{1y} = a\sin \alpha_1$;  
(1.5) $w_{2x} = a\cos \alpha_{2} - a_{0}$;  
(1.6) $w_{2y} = -a\sin \alpha_{2}$.

The equations of motion for the blocks and for the prism are:

(1.7) $m_{1}\mathbf{w}_{1} = m_{1}\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{R}_{1} + \mathbf{T}_{1}$;  
(1.8) $m_{2}\mathbf{w}_{2} = m_{2}\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{R}_{2} + \mathbf{T}_{2}$;  
(1.9) $M\mathbf{a}_0 = M\mathbf{g} - \mathbf{R}_1 - \mathbf{R}_2 + \mathbf{R} - \mathbf{T}_1 - \mathbf{T}_2$.

The forces of tension $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ at the ends of the thread are of the same magnitude $T$ since the masses of the thread and that of the pulley are negligible. Projecting along $x$ and $y$ gives:

$$
m_{1} a \cos \alpha_{1} - m_{1} a_{0} = T \cos \alpha_{1} - R_{1} \sin \alpha_{1}; \tag{1.10}
$$

$$
m_{1} a \sin \alpha_{1} = T \sin \alpha_{1} + R_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} - m_{1} g; \tag{1.11}
$$

$$
m_{2} a \cos \alpha_{2} - m_{2} a_{0} = - T \cos \alpha_{2} + R_{2} \sin \alpha_{2}; \tag{1.12}
$$

$$
m_{2} a \sin \alpha_{2} = T \sin \alpha_{2} + R_{2} \sin \alpha_{2} - m_{2} g; \tag{1.13}
$$

$$
- M a_{0} = R_{1} \sin \alpha_{1} - R_{2} \sin \alpha_{2} - T \cos \alpha_{1} + T \cos \alpha_{2}; \tag{1.14}
$$

$$
0 = R - R_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} - R_{2} \cos \alpha_{2} - M g. \tag{1.15}
$$

Adding equations (1.10), (1.12), and (1.14) cancels all internal forces, yielding the relation between $a$ and $a_0$:

$$
a = a_{0} \frac{M + m_{1} + m_{2}}{m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2}}. \tag{1.16}
$$

Eliminating the unknown forces gives the final answer for $a_{0}$:

$$
a_{0} = \frac{(m_{1} \sin \alpha_{1} - m_{2} \sin \alpha_{2})(m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2})}{(m_{1} + m_{2} + M)(m_{1} + m_{2}) - (m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2})^{2}}. \tag{1.17}
$$

It follows from equation (1.17) that the prism will be in equilibrium $(a_0 = 0)$ if:

$$
\frac{m_{1}}{m_{2}} = \frac{\sin \alpha_{2}}{\sin \alpha_{1}}. \tag{1.18}
$$

\boxed{a = a_{0} \frac{M + m_{1} + m_{2}}{m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2}}}

The blocks slide relative to the prism with accelerations $a_{1}$ and $a_{2}$, which are parallel to its sides and have the same magnitude $a$. The blocks move relative to the earth with accelerations:

(1.1) $\mathbf{w}_1 = \mathbf{a}_1 + \mathbf{a}_0$;  
(1.2) $\mathbf{w}_2 = \mathbf{a}_2 + \mathbf{a}_0$.

Projecting $\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\mathbf{w}_2$ along the $x$- and $y$-axes:

(1.3) $w_{1x} = a\cos \alpha_{1} - a_{0}$;  
(1.4) $w_{1y} = a\sin \alpha_1$;  
(1.5) $w_{2x} = a\cos \alpha_{2} - a_{0}$;  
(1.6) $w_{2y} = -a\sin \alpha_{2}$.

The equations of motion for the blocks and for the prism are:

(1.7) $m_{1}\mathbf{w}_{1} = m_{1}\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{R}_{1} + \mathbf{T}_{1}$;  
(1.8) $m_{2}\mathbf{w}_{2} = m_{2}\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{R}_{2} + \mathbf{T}_{2}$;  
(1.9) $M\mathbf{a}_0 = M\mathbf{g} - \mathbf{R}_1 - \mathbf{R}_2 + \mathbf{R} - \mathbf{T}_1 - \mathbf{T}_2$.

The forces of tension $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ at the ends of the thread are of the same magnitude $T$ since the masses of the thread and that of the pulley are negligible. Projecting the equations of motion along $x$ and $y$ gives:

$$
m_{1} a \cos \alpha_{1} - m_{1} a_{0} = T \cos \alpha_{1} - R_{1} \sin \alpha_{1}; \tag{1.10}
$$

$$
m_{1} a \sin \alpha_{1} = T \sin \alpha_{1} + R_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} - m_{1} g; \tag{1.11}
$$

$$
m_{2} a \cos \alpha_{2} - m_{2} a_{0} = - T \cos \alpha_{2} + R_{2} \sin \alpha_{2}; \tag{1.12}
$$

$$
m_{2} a \sin \alpha_{2} = T \sin \alpha_{2} + R_{2} \sin \alpha_{2} - m_{2} g; \tag{1.13}
$$

$$
- M a_{0} = R_{1} \sin \alpha_{1} - R_{2} \sin \alpha_{2} - T \cos \alpha_{1} + T \cos \alpha_{2}; \tag{1.14}
$$

$$
0 = R - R_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} - R_{2} \cos \alpha_{2} - M g. \tag{1.15}
$$

Adding equations (1.10), (1.12), and (1.14) cancels all internal forces, yielding the relation between $a$ and $a_0$:

$$
a = a_{0} \frac{M + m_{1} + m_{2}}{m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2}}. \tag{1.16}
$$

Eliminating the unknown forces gives the final answer for $a_{0}$:

$$
a_{0} = \frac{(m_{1} \sin \alpha_{1} - m_{2} \sin \alpha_{2})(m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2})}{(m_{1} + m_{2} + M)(m_{1} + m_{2}) - (m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2})^{2}}. \tag{1.17}
$$

It follows from equation (1.17) that the prism will be in equilibrium $(a_0 = 0)$ if:

$$
\frac{m_{1}}{m_{2}} = \frac{\sin \alpha_{2}}{\sin \alpha_{1}}. \tag{1.18}
$$

\boxed{a_{0} = \frac{(m_{1} \sin \alpha_{1} - m_{2} \sin \alpha_{2})(m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2})}{(m_{1} + m_{2} + M)(m_{1} + m_{2}) - (m_{1} \cos \alpha_{1} + m_{2} \cos \alpha_{2})^{2}}}

The blocks slide relative to the prism with accelerations $a_1$ and $a_2$, which are parallel to its sides and have the same magnitude $a$. The blocks move relative to the earth with accelerations:

(1.1) $\mathbf{w}_1 = \mathbf{a}_1 + \mathbf{a}_0$;  
(1.2) $\mathbf{w}_2 = \mathbf{a}_2 + \mathbf{a}_0$.

Projecting $\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\mathbf{w}_2$ along the $x$- and $y$-axes:

(1.3) $w_{1x} = a\cos \alpha_1 - a_0$;  
(1.4) $w_{1y} = a\sin \alpha_1$;  
(1.5) $w_{2x} = a\cos \alpha_2 - a_0$;  
(1.6) $w_{2y} = -a\sin \alpha_2$.

The equations of motion for the blocks and for the prism are:

(1.7) $m_1\mathbf{w}_1 = m_1\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{R}_1 + \mathbf{T}_1$;  
(1.8) $m_2\mathbf{w}_2 = m_2\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{R}_2 + \mathbf{T}_2$;  
(1.9) $M\mathbf{a}_0 = M\mathbf{g} - \mathbf{R}_1 - \mathbf{R}_2 + \mathbf{R} - \mathbf{T}_1 - \mathbf{T}_2$.

The forces of tension $T_1$ and $T_2$ at the ends of the thread are of the same magnitude $T$ since the masses of the thread and that of the pulley are negligible. Projecting along $x$ and $y$ gives:

$$
m_1 a \cos \alpha_1 - m_1 a_0 = T \cos \alpha_1 - R_1 \sin \alpha_1; \tag{1.10}
$$

$$
m_1 a \sin \alpha_1 = T \sin \alpha_1 + R_1 \cos \alpha_1 - m_1 g; \tag{1.11}
$$

$$
m_2 a \cos \alpha_2 - m_2 a_0 = -T \cos \alpha_2 + R_2 \sin \alpha_2; \tag{1.12}
$$

$$
m_2 a \sin \alpha_2 = T \sin \alpha_2 + R_2 \sin \alpha_2 - m_2 g; \tag{1.13}
$$

$$
- M a_0 = R_1 \sin \alpha_1 - R_2 \sin \alpha_2 - T \cos \alpha_1 + T \cos \alpha_2; \tag{1.14}
$$

$$
0 = R - R_1 \cos \alpha_1 - R_2 \cos \alpha_2 - M g. \tag{1.15}
$$

Adding equations (1.10), (1.12), and (1.14) cancels all internal forces, yielding:

$$
a = a_0 \frac{M + m_1 + m_2}{m_1 \cos \alpha_1 + m_2 \cos \alpha_2}. \tag{1.16}
$$

Eliminating the unknown forces gives the acceleration $a_0$:

$$
a_0 = \frac{(m_1 \sin \alpha_1 - m_2 \sin \alpha_2)(m_1 \cos \alpha_1 + m_2 \cos \alpha_2)}{(m_1 + m_2 + M)(m_1 + m_2) - (m_1 \cos \alpha_1 + m_2 \cos \alpha_2)^2}. \tag{1.17}
$$

For the prism to be in equilibrium ($a_0 = 0$), the numerator must vanish, so:

$$
\frac{m_1}{m_2} = \frac{\sin \alpha_2}{\sin \alpha_1}. \tag{1.18}
$$

\boxed{\frac{m_1}{m_2} = \frac{\sin \alpha_2}{\sin \alpha_1}}

