The resolving power of the microscope (in the meaning mentioned in the problem) is proportional to the wavelength, in this case to the de Broglie wavelength:

$$
\lambda = \frac{h}{p}
$$

where $h$ is Planck's constant and $p$ is the momentum of the particle. The wavelength is inversely proportional to the momentum. Therefore, after replacing the electron beam with a proton beam, the resolving power changes by the factor $p/p' \approx 1/35$. This means that the proton microscope would allow observation of objects about 35 times smaller than the electron microscope.

\boxed{\text{The resolving power increases by a factor of about 35.}}

