The momentum of the atom after absorption in the laboratory frame is given by the initial momentum of the atom plus the momentum of the absorbed photon. The photon momentum is $\hbar q$, where $q = \omega_L / c$ is the wave number. Thus,

$$p_{at} = p - \hbar q \approx mv - \frac{\hbar \omega_L}{c}.$$

\boxed{p_{at} = mv - \frac{\hbar \omega_L}{c}}

The total energy of the atom after absorption, in the laboratory frame, is the sum of its kinetic energy and its internal excitation energy:

\[
\varepsilon_{at} = \frac{p_{at}^{2}}{2m} + \hbar\omega_{0}
\]

where $p_{at}$ is the momentum of the atom after absorption, $m$ is its mass, and $\hbar\omega_{0}$ is the internal energy difference.  For a slowly moving atom, $p_{at} \approx mv$, so

\[
\varepsilon_{at} \approx \frac{mv^{2}}{2} + \hbar\omega_{L}
\]

where $\omega_{L}$ is the laser frequency (since the absorbed photon energy is $\hbar\omega_{L}$).

\boxed{\varepsilon_{at} = \frac{p_{at}^{2}}{2m} + \hbar\omega_{0}}

The average momentum of the atom after emission is given by the weighted average of the two possible momenta:

\[ p_{at} = \frac{1}{2} p_{at}^{+} + \frac{1}{2} p_{at}^{-} \approx p - \frac{\hbar \omega_{L}}{c} \]

\boxed{p_{at} = p - \frac{\hbar \omega_{L}}{c}}

The average momentum change of the atom after a complete one-photon absorption-emission process is given by

\[ \Delta p = p_{\text{at}}^{\text{after}} - p_{\text{at}}^{\text{before}} \approx +\hbar q = +\frac{\hbar \omega_L'}{c}. \]

\boxed{\Delta p = +\frac{\hbar \omega_L'}{c}}

