A spiral trajectory occurs when the condition for a real minimum distance of approach fails. The minimum distance $r_{\min}$ is real under the condition:

$$
1 \geq \frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2 b^4}
$$

This gives the critical impact parameter:

$$
b \geq b_0 = \left(\frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2}\right)^{1/4}
$$

For $b < b_0$, the ion will descend along a spiral and collide with the atom, leading to charge exchange. The cross-sectional area for this process is:

$$
A = \pi b_0^2 = \pi \left(\frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2}\right)^{1/2}
$$

\boxed{A = \pi \left(\frac{\alpha Q^2}{4\pi^2 \varepsilon_0^2 m v_0^2}\right)^{1/2}}

The stone trajectory is reversible. By energy conservation, the problem is equivalent to finding the minimal initial speed for a stone thrown from the topmost point of the spherical building down to the ground without hitting the roof. The optimal trajectory must touch the roof at some point (other than the topmost point). If it did not touch the roof, one could keep the same launch angle and reduce the speed slightly, and the stone would still reach the ground without hitting the roof. If the trajectory were tangent at the topmost point, the curvature of the parabola is maximum there, so it would not touch or intersect the roof elsewhere. Then one could keep the speed constant and increase the launch angle slightly; the new trajectory would not be tangent at the top nor touch the roof, allowing a further reduction in speed. Hence the optimal trajectory touches the roof at some point, as sketched below.

![Optimal trajectory sketch](images/15f2633aefb03dc773b4228b143a9230bcb64b4f53020dfb27e3ad27a27ceec1.jpg)

