\boxed{d_w \approx 4 \times 10^{-10} \, \mathrm{m}}

Using the inequality $P \ll a/V^{2}$, the van der Waals equation of state is written as

$$
\frac{a}{V_{L}^{2}} (V_{L} - b) = R T, \tag{B4.1}
$$

whose solution is

$$
V_{L} = \frac{a}{2 R T} \left(1 \pm \sqrt{1 - \frac{4 b R T}{a}}\right). \tag{B4.2}
$$

In this case, the smaller root should be taken, since at $T \to 0$ the liquid volume $V_{L} = b$ must be obtained according to (B4.1), i.e.

$$
V_{L} = \frac{a}{2 R T} \left(1 - \sqrt{1 - \frac{4 b R T}{a}}\right) \approx b \left(1 + \frac{b R T}{a}\right). \tag{B4.3}
$$

\boxed{V_{L} = \frac{a}{2 R T} \left(1 - \sqrt{1 - \frac{4 b R T}{a}}\right)}

Since (B4.3) gives the volume of one mole of water, its mass density is easily found as

$$
\rho_{L} = \frac{\mu}{V_{L}} = \frac{\mu}{b \left(1 + \frac{b R T}{a}\right)} \approx \frac{\mu}{b} = 5.8 \cdot 10^{2} \frac{\mathrm{kg}}{\mathrm{m}^{3}}
$$

\boxed{\rho_{L} = \frac{\mu}{b \left(1 + \frac{b R T}{a}\right)} \approx \frac{\mu}{b} = 5.8 \cdot 10^{2} \frac{\mathrm{kg}}{\mathrm{m}^{3}}}

In accordance with (B4.3) the volume thermal expansion coefficient is derived as

$$
\alpha = \frac{1}{V_L} \frac{\Delta V_L}{\Delta T} = \frac{bR}{a + bRT} \approx \frac{\bar{b}R}{a} = 4.6 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{K}^{-1}
$$

\boxed{\alpha = \frac{bR}{a + bRT} \approx 4.6 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{K}^{-1}}

The heat required to convert the liquid to gas is used to overcome the intermolecular forces that create negative pressure $a/V^{2}$. Therefore,

$$
E = L \mu \approx \int_{V_L}^{V_G} \frac{a}{V^2} \, dV = a \left(\frac{1}{V_L} - \frac{1}{V_G}\right) \tag{B7.1}
$$

Using $V_G \gg V_L$, (B7.1) yields

$$
L = \frac{a}{\mu V_L} = \frac{a}{\mu b \left(1 + \frac{b R T}{a}\right)} \approx \frac{a}{\mu b} = 1.0 \times 10^6 \, \frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{kg}} \tag{B7.2}
$$

\boxed{L = \frac{a}{\mu b} = 1.0 \times 10^6 \, \frac{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{kg}}}

By definition of the secondary electron emission coefficient, the following condition should be imposed:

